course: dictionary
lesson: 1306
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the words around it show you which one the writer means. Cable is a good example. It can mean a thick, strong wire or rope, or a set of wires inside a cover that carries electricity, telephone calls, TV or internet.
explain: «When something doesn't work, test the simple causes first: is the socket switched on, is the cable in?» Socket and "is it in" are the clues. Here cable means a wire with a plug on the end. Try your meaning in the sentence to check it still makes sense.
explain: «Tall buildings became possible with steel frames and with safe lifts, shown by Elisha Otis in the 1850s, which stop the lift falling if a cable breaks.» Here the clues are lift and falling. A cable is the thick, strong wire that holds the lift up.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: A cable is a thick, strong wire or rope, or a set of wires in a cover that carries electricity, telephone calls, TV or internet.
fact: Cable TV is television that comes through cables.
fact: Electric cables are made of copper wire covered with plastic, which does not carry electricity.
fact: Never touch a damaged cable or pull a plug by its cable.
exercise: «Workers spray coloured paint marks where pipes and cables run under the pavement, so the diggers do not hit them.» What is a cable in this sentence? | A type of pavement | A hungry animal that digs | A paint brush | *A wire or set of wires hidden under the ground
exercise: Find and copy one word from «Mice use pipes, cables and gaps behind kitchen units as hidden roads» that means wires inside a cover. | gaps | units | pipes | *cables
exercise: What is cable TV? | A TV with no picture | Television that runs on batteries only | *Television that comes through cables | A film about cats
test: «Safe lifts stop the lift falling if a cable breaks.» In this sentence, what does cable mean? | *A thick, strong wire that holds up the lift | A type of lift button | A bag for shopping | A sort of sweet
test: Why must you never pull a plug by its cable? | Because plugs need a loud noise to work | Because the plug will taste bad | *Because you can damage the wire inside and hurt yourself | Because cables are made of sugar
exam: In an old book, «She cabled her brother the news.» What does cabled mean? | Painted a wall | *Sent a message by telegram | Tied up with rope | Ate a meal
